// ── BUCKET 3, BLESSED DIVERGENCE — NOT RE-PORTED, AND NOT A DEFECT. 2026-09-07.
// Original path: tests/ui/borrowck/borrowck-move-out-of-tuple-struct-with-dtor.rs
// upstream commit: 4b0c9d76ae7d387229caea55cfa73c280b08b8a7
// upstream construct: lines 8-22 — `S(_s)` in match / let / fn-arg where `impl Drop for S`; //~ ERROR cannot move out of type `S`, which implements the `Drop` trait
// Modifications: none — NOT PORTED AS-IS AND NOT PORTABLE AS-IS. The construct
//   cannot be made illegal here: Logos deliberately ADMITS a move out of a field
//   of a value with a user `Drop`, and defines what its destructor then does —
//   `intrinsic.drop.skip-moved-out-paths` (docs/spec/expressions.md), "Drop of a
//   value suppresses sub-values that were moved out: a dotted path whose segment
//   exactly matches a child skips that child's drop entirely". E0509 exists in
//   Rust precisely because Rust has no such rule.
// ⚠ THE CLAUSE WAS RE-VERIFIED BY RUNNING IT, 2026-09-07, not by reading it: a
//   program with `struct S { f: Inner }`, `impl Drop` on BOTH, `let g = s.f;`
//   and a static drop counter per type prints INNER_DROPS=1 S_DROPS=1 —
//   `S::drop` runs once, the moved-out field is dropped once through `g`, and
//   the owner skips it. No leak, no double free. The divergence is implemented,
//   not merely declared.
// The file stays on the admit shelf and keeps its row in
// tests/logos/bc_admits_blocked.ledger; it is not a hole and never was.
package borrowck_move_out_of_tuple_struct_with_dtor_t13;
struct Inner { a: i64 }
struct S { f: Inner }
impl Drop for S { fn drop(self: &mut S) {} }
fn main() -> i32 {
    let s = S { f: Inner { a: 1i64 } };
    let S { f: inner } = s;
    return inner.a as i32;
}
